// The scene's light sources. See include/custom/SceneLights.hpp for the three
// types and what every field means.
//
//   direct   direction + color. No position, no decay.
//   point    position + color + decay (g, beta).
//   spot     a point light plus an aim direction and two cone angles.
//
//   g        distance at which the light is exactly its stated color
//   beta     falloff exponent: 0 constant, 1 inverse-linear, 2 inverse-square
//
// Positions are world coordinates, and "instance" + "offset" hangs a light off a
// scene.json instance instead of repeating its coordinates.
{
	// Indirect lighting. A diffuse surface's indirect light is "bounce" alone
	// now: the hemispheric sky/ground split (E07 s.47-54) made sense outdoors
	// with a sun, but the sun is gone (see the empty "lights" array below) and
	// this dungeon is sealed -- no window, no grate, no sky -- so a term that
	// claims light arrives from "above" and "below" independent of any actual
	// source had degenerated into exactly the flat constant the project rules
	// say barely counts as indirect lighting. "bounce" doesn't have that
	// problem: it reads each point/spot light's own radiance and shadow
	// visibility (CookTorrance.frag), so a corridor with no torch in it
	// collects nothing, the way an unlit dungeon corridor actually looks.
	//
	// "upper"/"lower"/"direction" are now UNUSED -- CookTorrance.frag no
	// longer reads them, metals included: metalAmbient() was rewired to
	// reflect the same torch bounce everything else gets (through Fresnel),
	// rather than sampling a separately authored two-color environment, so a
	// metal can't show ambient light the actual torches/candles didn't put
	// there. Left authored here rather than deleted, since removing them
	// means touching AmbientLight's parsing and the global UBO layout for
	// zero behavior change. Safe to ignore.
	//
	// "weight" (aw) is the blend share CookTorrance.frag gives the whole
	// ambient bucket against the direct term: direct keeps (1-weight), ambient
	// takes weight. Bumped from E17's enclosed-maze value of 0.05 now that
	// bounce is carrying the mandatory-indirect-lighting requirement alone --
	// 0.05 made it nearly invisible even right next to a lit torch. Treat this
	// as a starting point to tune by eye, not a derived constant.
	//
	// "bounce" is a share of "weight", not an addition to it (turning the
	// Torch Bounce cheat off removes it from the bucket entirely). 0.35 is an
	// authored stand-in for "how much of what hits a stone wall comes back" --
	// plausible for a matte masonry albedo -- and is wrapped rather than
	// clamped ((dot+1)/2, no terminator) because bounced light arrives from
	// most of the hemisphere, not from the flame's own direction. It is
	// shadowed by the same visibility as the direct term: a fragment that
	// cannot see the flame shouldn't collect a fill sized by its distance to
	// it.
	"ambient": {
		"upper": [0.75, 0.42, 0.18],
		"lower": [0.45, 0.26, 0.12],
		"direction": [0.0, 1.0, 0.0],
		"weight": 0.12,
		"bounce": 0.35
	},

	"lights": [
		// The sun is GONE. It used to sit here as the scene's one "direct"
		// light, parked ~55 degrees above the horizon, tinted warm, casting
		// through the single 2D shadow map:
		//
		//   { "id": "sun", "type": "direct",
		//     "direction": [0.25, -0.82, -0.52], "color": [1.0, 0.98, 0.92],
		//     "orbitSpeed": 0.0, "castsShadow": true }
		//
		// Kept here as a comment because putting it back is one paste: nothing
		// in the engine was removed with it. SceneLights still loads "direct"
		// lights, main.cpp still builds the sun's orthographic light-space
		// matrix for any light that asks for a shadow (SUN_TARGET /
		// SUN_ORTHO_HALF_EXTENT there), and the 2D shadow slots are still
		// allocated -- they just go unused now, since no light in this file
		// claims one.
		//
		// Its indirect half went with it: see "upper"/"lower" above, both black.
		//
		// The dungeon wall torches (torchW1/W2, torchE1/E2, torchDC, and the
		// dv alcove's torchDV) used to be authored here too, hung off their
		// instances with "instance" + "offset". They were removed: every one of
		// them also carries the "dungeonTorch" model, so flames.json spawns a
		// TorchFlame for each of them independently (main.cpp's "Torch
		// flames' point lights" block), which appends its own per-frame point
		// light into gubo already -- correct position even while the flame
		// billboard sways, flicker, and a shot at the dynamic shadow-cube
		// pool. Keeping a second, static copy here just doubled each torch's
		// light contribution and doubled its debug gizmo (two crosses per
		// torch, and since only the TorchFlame copy is distance-culled, only
		// one of the pair would disappear when walking away). TorchFlame is
		// now the sole owner of these six; nothing else in this file
		// duplicates a flame-driven instance.
	]
}
